2.6 Backend Diagnostics And Ownership
=====================================

Use the boundary at which speech failed to choose the next diagnostic:

‘omnivox’ is not found
     Check ‘PATH’; on POSIX, an absolute ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ can identify
     the installed executable.  Keep its adjacent ‘espeak-ng-data’ and
     ‘third-party-licenses’ directories with it.
‘omnivox --check’ reports an error or is inaudible
     The failure is below Emacsvox.  Consult Omnivox's build guide
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md),
     platform status
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/STATUS.md), and
     diagnostics guide
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/DIAGNOSTICS.md).
     Do not diagnose Emacs configuration until the tone and speech check
     passes.
The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
     Confirm that ‘TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox’ was present before Emacsvox
     loaded, then use ‘M-x tts-speak-version’.  Under WSL2, also run
     ‘make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime’ and restart the speech
     process after restaging.
A process starts and later fails
     The Emacsvox POSIX/WSL launcher writes private, bounded stderr logs
     below ‘$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox’, defaulting to
     ‘~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox’.  Spoken text is excluded unless
     ‘OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT’ is explicitly enabled; enabling it can
     expose passwords, messages, and document contents.

   Emacsvox owns server selection, its POSIX/WSL launcher, Windows
staging, and the bundled Lisp adapter.  Omnivox owns its native builds,
engines, command line, runtime data layout, and protocol specifications.
Backend-local READMEs own licensed-runtime and compatibility-adapter
details.  Cross-link these owners rather than copying their versioned
contracts into installation instructions.


