3 Basic Usage.
**************

This chapter gives a short, task-oriented introduction to Emacsvox.  For
the complete generated key reference, see *note
(emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps::.  Use ‘C-h m’ to inspect the
modes and bindings active in the current buffer, or ‘C-h k’ followed by
a key to ask Emacs what that key does.  These sections describe the
speech-enabling extensions, but are not a substitute for the Emacs
manual.

   All Emacs navigation and editing commands have been speech enabled.
Thus, moving to the next or previous word, line or paragraph results in
the text around point being spoken.  Exactly how much text is spoken is
determined by the amount by which you moved.

   In addition, Emacsvox provides basic reading functions that can be
invoked to listen to chunks of text without moving.

* Menu:

* Overview::                     Basic Overview.
* Working::                      Working in Emacsvox.
* Reading::                      Reading chunks of information.
* Speech System::                Controlling Speech Output.
* Voice-lock::                   Audio Formatting.
* Status Information::           Speaking Status Information.


